{"name":"Angel Shark","hex":"#e19640","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"angel shark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the unusual genus Squatina of sharks with flattened bodies and broad pectoral fins resembling skates or rays. There are approximately 16 known species, which are the only members of their taxonomic genus, family and order. They occur worldwide in temperate and tropical seas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/angel_shark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Angelshark","blocks":[{"text":"Angel sharks are sharks belonging to the genus Squatina. They are the only living members of the family Squatinidae and order Squatiniformes. They commonly inhabit sandy seabeds close to 150 m (490 ft) in depth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Squatina_australis.jpg/330px-Squatina_australis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angelshark","wikidata":"Q756731"},{"title":"Angel","blocks":[{"text":"An angel is a spiritual, heavenly, or supernatural entity, often depicted as humanoid with bird-like wings, frequently in the role as a messenger or intermediary between God and humanity in various traditions like the Abrahamic religions. Other roles include protectors and guides for humans, such as guardian angels, and servants of God. In Western belief-systems the term is often used to distinguish benevolent from malevolent intermediary beings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Angel_of_the_Annunciation_MET_DP167921.jpg/330px-Angel_of_the_Annunciation_MET_DP167921.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angel","wikidata":"Q235113"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark","wikidata":"Q7372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Squatina_dumeril_nefsc2.jpg/500px-Squatina_dumeril_nefsc2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Squatina_dumeril_nefsc2.jpg/500px-Squatina_dumeril_nefsc2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASquatina_dumeril_nefsc2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1c/Conserved_neurocranium_shapein_AngelSharks_Fig1_HTML.webp/500px-Conserved_neurocranium_shapein_AngelSharks_Fig1_HTML.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/1c/Conserved_neurocranium_shapein_AngelSharks_Fig1_HTML.webp/1280px-Conserved_neurocranium_shapein_AngelSharks_Fig1_HTML.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","sr